  "content": {
    "id": "p22-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "Let $f:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be a function. Suppose $f\\circ g=g\\circ f$ for all functions $g:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$. Prove that $f(x)=x$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "If $f(x_0)=y_0\\ne x_0$, choose $g$ with $g(x_0)=x_0$ and $g(y_0)\\ne y_0$. Then $f(g(x_0))=y_0\\ne g(f(x_0))$, contradicting $f\\circ g=g\\circ f$. Thus $f(x)=x$ for every $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.",
      "worked": "Suppose there is an $x_0\\in\\mathbb{R}$ such that $f(x_0)=y_0\\ne x_0$. Since $x_0$ and $y_0$ are distinct, choose a function $g:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ with $g(x_0)=x_0$ and $g(y_0)\\ne y_0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f(g(x_0))&=f(x_0)\\\\f(g(x_0))&=y_0\\\\g(f(x_0))&=g(y_0)\\ne y_0.\\end{align*}$$\n\nHence $f(g(x_0))\\ne g(f(x_0))$, contradicting the hypothesis. Therefore $f(x)=x$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 48
  },
